<p>Lebanese Christians marked Easter this year with celebrations shaped by the war along Lebanon’s southern border, where fighting between Israel and Hezbollah has displaced families and left some villages hard to reach. In Beirut and other safer areas, worshippers said their prayers were dedicated to people in the south, reflecting both grief over disrupted traditions and solidarity across communities affected by the conflict. For many families, Easter unfolded far from home churches and hometown gatherings because displacement has upended daily life and religious customs in southern Christian villages. Even so, the reporting points to resilience: despite separation and insecurity, church services continued, and worshippers used the holiday to express unity with those still living under the shadow of cross-border violence.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IWYgcif3s7cS19U1fBOhIEWcmCGse-kNZwQesMANH7q4gBOKHq8RGCIFA7H4Ui2qVR1wpqDlHW4HU73cT2GU7j8VRsBYWKoAi7QomrjHxnwV4oulPO_Q9r3Mx8QfXXw4CpT2G0s_ce4BOvkVHfZprd_0S0hVKrjbPT1-SnSDHQ' alt='Lebanese Christians marking Easter during wartime displacement' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>South focus: At a church in Jdeideh, parish choir leader Jenny Yazbek al-Jamal said the celebrations were dedicated to “people in the south,” adding that suffering in this war is not limited to Christian villages alone.</li><li>Border strain: Fighting between Israel and Hezbollah has trapped affected southern villages in ongoing violence, kept residents from their homes, and disrupted normal religious observances during one of Christianity’s most important holidays.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Displaced Christian families: Many marked Easter away from their homes and churches after the war forced them to leave, showing how directly the conflict has reshaped family life and religious practice. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/lebanese-christians-displaced-by-war-mark-easter-far-from-their-homes-and-churches'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Jenny Yazbek al-Jamal: She said her family lives in the south and that she feels personally connected to those enduring the conflict there, while stressing that the war’s suffering reaches beyond Christian communities. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/lebanons-christians-mark-easter-solidarity-war-hit-south'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Worshippers in Beirut: Churchgoers used Easter services to direct prayers and public solidarity toward southern communities still living with the effects of border fighting. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/lebanons-christians-mark-easter-in-solidarity-with-war-hit-south'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/lebanese-christians-displaced-by-war-mark-easter-far-from-their-homes-and-churches'>Lebanese Christians displaced by war mark Easter far from their homes and churches</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/lebanons-christians-mark-easter-in-solidarity-with-war-hit-south'>Lebanon’s Christians mark Easter in solidarity with war-hit south</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/lebanons-christians-mark-easter-solidarity-war-hit-south'>Lebanon&#x27;s Christians mark Easter in solidarity with war-hit south</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/displaced-war-lebanons-christians-mark-easter-homes-churches-131742006'>Displaced by war, Lebanon&#x27;s Christians mark Easter far from their homes and churches</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>